# Toccata in D minor, from the Toccata and Fugue BWV 565 (attributed to
# J. S. Bach)
#
# The Toccata alone: the Fugue is too big for the jukebox for now. The
# work survives only in a later copy (Johannes Ringk) and its
# attribution to Bach has been questioned, though it is still catalogued
# as his.
#
# Character: an organ toccata in the north German "stylus phantasticus":
# free, rhapsodic, theatrical. The famous opening mordent and the
# octave run down to a diminished seventh over a low D; prestissimo
# flourishes; a moto passage of sixteenths around a repeated A; chords
# echoed by the pedal; triplet waves over a diminished chord; a pedal
# solo and the closing cadence. The score's own marks: Adagio (bar 1),
# Prestissimo (bar 4 and bar 22). The Adagissimo, Presto and Molto
# Adagio marks in the Mutopia file belong to the Fugue's coda (bars
# 127-143), not to the Toccata.
#
# Time signature: 4/4 throughout. The Mutopia MIDI is the whole work,
# 143 bars. The Toccata ends with the D minor chord on the first half of
# bar 30; the Fugue's subject enters in the left hand on beat 3 of bar
# 30 (score and MIDI agree). `play 1-30.5` takes the Toccata only.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 76 as the base, shaped section by section (below).
# MusicBrainz track times for the Toccata alone: Ewald Kooiman 2:25;
# Ton Koopman 2:31 (a release that splits the work); Michel Chapuis 2:33;
# Helmut Walcha 2:36 (library listing); Wolfgang Ruebsam 2:38; Herbert
# Collum 2:42; Piet Kee 2:45; Hans Fagius 2:59. About 2:30-2:45, an
# average of only about 45 quarters a minute over the 118 beats, because
# so much of the time is in the fermatas, the held chords, the silences
# and the church's echo. A single tempo cannot give that: the base is
# set by the running passages and every free section is broadened.
# 76 keeps the prestissimo triplet sixteenths at about 4 frames each,
# the fastest that still sounds as notes on the chip (judgment, not a
# measured figure). The result runs about 2:10: the chip has no
# reverberant church to fill the silences, so I did not stretch them to
# the recordings' length.
#
# Structure (score bars; Mutopia, read against the MIDI):
#   1-3    Adagio: the mordent on A (twice, octaves apart), the octave
#          run down to the diminished seventh over the low pedal D
#          (bar 2), resolving to D (bar 3)
#   4-7    Prestissimo: rising triplet figures, each pair ending on a
#          fermata rest (bars 5 and 7)
#   8-10   falling triplet sequence to the diminished seventh over D
#   11-12  a flourish down and up (bar 11), fermata on D (bar 12, beat 1)
#   12-15  sixteenths in the right hand against a repeated A in the
#          left: the moto passage, falling through D minor
#   16-17  chords answered by the pedal (B-flat, A)
#   18-21  rhapsodic 32nd-note runs and chord echoes over the pedal,
#          to the A major chord with C-sharp and E (bar 22)
#   22-26  Prestissimo: triplet waves between the hands over the
#          diminished seventh (C-sharp, E, G, B-flat)
#   27-28  chords: A major, D minor, G minor, A7 (no tempo mark in
#          this edition; organists take them very broadly)
#   28-29  the pedal solo, down from B-flat to the low A
#   29-30  closing cadence; D minor chord on bar 30 (beats 1-2)
#
# Plan: terraced like organ registration: full organ for the opening
# and the prestissimo; a lighter manual for the moto passage; full again
# for the chord echoes and the second prestissimo; everything for the
# final chords. The free sections are broadened hard, as organists take
# them; the running passages keep the base tempo or press on. The left
# hand and right hand play in octaves at the start and the organ chords
# have up to seven notes, so some notes drop (58 of 940): the outer
# lines are kept.

tempo 76
play 1-30.5

voice mid 1        # keep inner chord notes behind the outer lines

dyn 1 ff           # Adagio and the first Prestissimo: full organ
dyn 12.25 mf       # bars 12-15: the moto passage, a lighter manual
dyn 16 f           # bars 16-21: chords and pedal echoes
dyn 22 f           # bars 22-26: the triplet waves
dyn 27 ff          # bars 27-30: the final chords and the pedal solo

lead 2-3 bottom    # the low pedal D under the diminished chord
lead 28-29 bottom  # the pedal solo

broaden 1-3 0.38   # Adagio: the mordents, the run, the held chord
                   # (about 29 quarters a minute, with the fermatas)
broaden 5 0.8      # fermata rests after the first triplet pair
broaden 7 0.8      # and after the second
broaden 10 0.8     # arrival on the diminished seventh over D
broaden 11.25 0.55 # one bar from beat 2 of bar 11: the flourish and the
                   # fermata D on beat 1 of bar 12
broaden 12.25-15 1.15  # the moto passage presses on (about 87)
broaden 16-17 0.8  # chords answered by the pedal
broaden 18-20 0.75 # the free 32nd-note runs
broaden 21 0.65    # into the chord of bar 22
broaden 27-28 0.5  # the closing chords (about 38)
broaden 29 0.75    # the end of the pedal solo and the cadence

rit 1.5 0.6        # the final cadence and the D minor chord
